# Project Context — AI-Assisted Red Teaming Framework ("Red Team Control Center")

Human-in-the-loop security assessment platform covering the FULL authorized-testing
lifecycle: operator imports a client engagement letter (PDF/DOCX/MD/TXT), the
framework parses scope/targets/rules deterministically, drafts a policy-checked
command plan, runs each scanner command ONLY after explicit human approval,
correlates findings, verifies them with letter-gated controlled exploitation,
collects bounded post-exploitation proof, and emits an HTML report. The phases
(scoping → recon → vuln analysis → exploitation → post-exploitation → reporting)
are visible in the UI stepper and drive the plan structure. Authorized lab use only.

## Architecture map

- `frontend/src/App.jsx` — the ENTIRE single-page React 19 UI (one file). `App.css` styles it.
- `backend/main.py` — FastAPI app: targets, assessments, execute, analyze, report,
  settings, `/engagement/parse`, `/requirements/extract`, `/capabilities`,
  `/assessments/{id}/phases`, `/assessments/{id}/phases/{phase}/plan`, `/health`.
- `backend/models.py` — Pydantic request schemas with bounds.
- `backend/database.py` — SQLAlchemy/SQLite ORM + migrations.
- `backend/modules/` — `engagement_parser` (letter → structured brief, deterministic,
  incl. per-target `exploitation_authorized` + `verification_endpoints`),
  `planner` (AI or default recon plan, policy-filtered), `exploit_planner`
  (findings → exploitation/post-exploitation steps, every step policy-validated),
  `next_steps` (adaptive next-step proposals — state digest → ranked candidates
  with verified finding provenance → policy-checked; ALWAYS proposes, NEVER
  persists. See "Tier 1: adaptive next-step proposals" below),
  `phases` (the phase model shared by backend and UI: PHASES, PLAN_PHASES,
  ANALYSIS_ADVANCES_TO, EXPLOITATION_GATED_TOOLS, PAYLOAD_FLAGS, VM_ONLY_TOOLS),
  `policy_engine` (allowlist, fail-closed, scope checks — THE security core; new
  `exploitation` capability group for sqlmap/msfconsole/searchsploit, curl payload
  flags gated on letter authorization, msfconsole `-x` scripts validated statement
  by statement: module tree allowlist, set-key allowlist, RHOSTS scope-checked),
  `executor` (async subprocess, 360s timeout, output caps, env sandbox; delegates
  to the VM engine when remote settings are passed), `ssh_executor` (Kali
  attacker-VM engine: paramiko SSH + tmux send-keys/capture-pane, per-run
  attestation; EXPECTED_TOOLS now includes sqlmap/searchsploit/msfconsole),
  `analyzer` (findings + scoring + sqlmap/searchsploit/msfconsole parsers +
  `match_verification` linking exploitation results to recon findings),
  `reporter` (Jinja2 HTML with phase summary + verification section),
  `secret_store` (Fernet encryption at rest).
- `docker-compose.yml` — backend :8000, frontend :5173 only (the assessor's
  toolkit). The backend maps `host.docker.internal` to `host-gateway` so Docker
  execution mode can reach services on the host (e.g. the OmniRoute gateway).
- Sample letter: `JuiceBox_Security_Assessment_Request.pdf` (letter v7, the
  real-application engagement `JB/SEC/2026/026`). The assessed asset is a real,
  self-hosted production app — the client's OmniRoute AI gateway (Node.js/
  Next.js, OpenAI-compatible API + dashboard) running on the assessment host
  at `192.168.198.86:20128` — plus the `192.168.198.0/24` lab segment for a
  discovery sweep. No letter target names a compose container.
  The v7 letter AUTHORIZES controlled vulnerability verification for the
  gateway only (Section 5.2: bounded PoC payloads, one benign verification
  record per flaw; a clean verification against the real app is a valid,
  expected outcome), declares `/v1/chat/completions` as its verification
  endpoint, and keeps brute-force / DoS / persistence / lateral movement
  prohibited.
- `backend/smoke_e2e.py` — one-shot end-to-end run against the live stack (letter →
  target → plan → execute every phase → analyze → exploit → verify → report),
  printing each result.

## Run / test

- Toolkit stack: `docker compose up --build` → UI at http://localhost:5173
  (the demo target — the OmniRoute gateway — runs on the host itself)
- Frontend checks: `cd frontend; npm run lint; npm run build`
- Backend tests: `cd backend; .\venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest -q` (188 tests)
- End-to-end: `.\backend\venv\Scripts\python.exe backend\smoke_e2e.py` (stack must be up)
- PowerShell gotcha: the path contains `[IMPLEMENTATION]`, so always use
  `Set-Location -LiteralPath "..."` (plain `cd` treats brackets as wildcards).

## Key decisions & gotchas

- CORS default allows BOTH `http://localhost:5173` and `http://127.0.0.1:5173`
  (port-forward links hand out 127.0.0.1). Override via `CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS`.
- Frontend `request()` helper: AbortController timeouts (75s general, 400s for execute —
  above the executor's 360s cap so the server decides how commands end), friendly
  network errors, FormData bodies skip the JSON header.
- UI polls `/health` every 10s → offline banner + auto-refresh on recovery.
- One global `busy` flag disables all buttons during requests; buttons label themselves
  ("Analyzing…", "Running…") so long requests read as progress, not dead UI.
- Scanner binaries (nmap, nuclei, …) exist ONLY inside the backend Docker image — a bare
  local Windows backend can drive UI/parser/planning but execution fails with a clear error.
- AI provider (OpenAI-compatible) is configured in the UI Configuration panel, stored
  Fernet-encrypted in the DB; never via env vars. Without it: deterministic fallbacks.
- Secrets are write-only: API responses return booleans, never values.
- Client letter restrictions (per-target tool bans) outrank both AI and operator plans.
- SQLite DB + secret key persist in the `data_volume` Docker volume (`down` keeps it,
  `down -v` wipes it).

## Phase-3 capabilities (current): the full phased lifecycle

- **Phases** (backend/modules/phases.py): scoping → recon → vuln_analysis →
  exploitation → post_exploitation → reporting. Plan steps carry a `phase` tag
  (untagged = recon); `Assessment.current_phase` + `analyzed_phases` drive the
  state machine. Analyzing a phase advances current_phase (recon →
  vuln_analysis, exploitation → post_exploitation, post_exploitation →
  reporting); `POST /assessments/{id}/phases/{phase}/plan` drafts the next
  phase FROM THE FINDINGS (409 unless the previous phase is analyzed, 403 if
  the letter does not authorize exploitation, 409 if the phase is already
  drafted). The UI renders a six-step phase stepper, groups the plan by phase
  with red accents on exploitation/post-exploition steps, and shows
  verification badges on findings.
- **Letter-gated exploitation**: `Target.exploitation_authorized` (parsed from
  the letter's controlled-verification sentences) gates the whole exploitation
  capability. The policy engine's `validate_command(..., allow_exploitation=)`
  refuses sqlmap/msfconsole/curl-with-body with "the letter does not authorize
  controlled exploitation" — at plan time (steps dropped from a supplied plan),
  at draft time (403 on the phase-plan endpoint) and at execute time (403).
- **Exploitation tools** (policy allowlists, all fail-closed):
  - `sqlmap` — conservative verification flags only (--batch, risk/level 1,
    technique B, bounded post-exploition facts --banner/--current-user/
    --current-db/--is-dba/--hostname). --os-shell/--dump/--sql-shell/
    --file-read/--all and every enumeration flag are refused.
  - `searchsploit` — offline Exploit-DB lookup (spec `offline: True`, so no
    explicit-target rule); -w/-p/-m/-x/--nmap refused.
  - `msfconsole` — VM-ONLY (local mode refuses at the execute endpoint with a
    clear 409). Driven with `-q -x "use …; set RHOSTS …; run; exit"`; the -x
    script is validated statement-by-statement: modules must be under
    auxiliary/scanner/ or exploit/{unix,linux,windows,multi}/, set keys are
    limited to RHOST/RHOSTS/RPORT/PORTS/THREADS/TIMEOUT/SSL/VERBOSE, and
    every RHOST/RHOSTS value must be in the authorized scopes.
  - `curl` payload flags (-d/--data*/-F/--form*/--json) are exploitation-grade
    and need the letter authorization; header-audit curl stays recon.
- **Exploit planner** (modules/exploit_planner.py): searchsploit steps per
  (service, version) fingerprint (cap 5), sqlmap verification per web endpoint
  (cap 3), curl PoC per letter-declared verification endpoint, msfconsole
  scanner steps for exposed mapped services; post-exploitation = one bounded
  fact (--banner / --current-user) per verified endpoint. Every step is
  policy-validated before it can be appended.
- **Verification**: analyzing the exploitation phase links its findings to the
  earlier phases' findings by endpoint/parameter (analyzer.match_verification),
  setting `verification`='verified'/'attempted', `exploit_evidence`, and
  `verified_by` on the recon finding — the report separates "a scanner matched
  a signature" from "exploitation proved it". Finding rows carry a `phase`
  column: re-analyzing one phase replaces only its own findings.
- Default deterministic plan: **7 policy-legal steps** (nmap/traceroute/dig/curl/whatweb/
  sslscan/nuclei), rate-limited (`--max-rate 30`, `-rl 30`), targets formatted as
  host:port with the scheme added exactly once.
- Deterministic analyzer: **per-tool parsers** (nmap service lines, nuclei template
  lines, curl header blocks incl. per-header missing-security-header findings +
  cookie flags, whatweb plugins, sslscan deprecated protocols, sqlmap injection
  confirmations, searchsploit rows, msfconsole scanner banners) with detailed
  explanations, endpoint/parameter, distinct scoring drivers, concrete remediation.
- **Subnet (CIDR) targets**: a target like `172.28.0.0/24` gets a 2-step discovery
  plan (`nmap -sn` live-host sweep, then `nmap -sV` over common ports), findings are
  attributed per host (`172.28.0.3:80/tcp`), and the policy engine only accepts a
  CIDR target that is equal to or a subnet of an authorized scope. /24 is the
  supported size — a /16 at the letter-mandated 30/s cannot finish inside the 360 s
  executor cap.
- Finding rows persist endpoint/parameter/exploitability/impact/exposure; assessments
  persist `analysis_mode`; both surfaced in UI and report.
- Live execution terminal: executor keeps an in-memory capped buffer per in-flight
  execution (`live_registry`), `GET /assessments/{id}/executions/{eid}/live` streams
  it, frontend polls every 1 s and renders a black terminal panel in the step card.
- Report: executive summary + severity bar, per-phase progress line, analysis-mode
  statement, exploitation & verification section, remediation summary table ordered
  by priority, scoring breakdown per finding, audit output, print stylesheet
  (Ctrl+P → clean PDF).
- Compose: toolkit only (backend + frontend). The demo target is NOT a compose
  service — the OmniRoute gateway runs on the host itself; Docker execution mode
  reaches it via `host.docker.internal:20128`.
- Letter v7 (`JB/SEC/2026/026`, real-application edition): criticalities 90/65,
  9 objectives (4.7 = controlled verification of critical findings), deny-list
  restrictions (no traceroute on the gateway, discovery-only on the segment),
  controlled-verification authorization for the gateway with
  `/v1/chat/completions` as the declared verification endpoint, rate-limit
  rules, and a second target authorizing the `192.168.198.0/24` lab LAN segment
  for a discovery sweep only. The primary target is a real self-hosted
  app (OmniRoute) on the assessment host, so a clean verification outcome in
  the exploitation phase is a valid, expected result.

## Tier 1: adaptive next-step proposals (the loop, with the human retained)

The engagement loop used to be open: `planner.generate_plan` runs ONCE at
assessment creation and never sees tool output, and the later phases are drafted
by deterministic rule from findings. `next_steps` closes it — but as an advisor,
not an actor.

- **Endpoint**: `POST /assessments/{id}/next-steps`. No request body; the phase
  is derived server-side from `current_phase`. **Persists nothing.** Precedent
  for a non-persisting POST: `/engagement/parse`, `/settings/ssh-test`.
- **Response**: `{phase, source, state{findings,executed_steps,plan_steps,headroom},
  candidates[], refused[], notes[]}`. `refused` is a FEATURE — every candidate
  the policy engine knocked back, with its reason. It is the guardrail's
  receipt, and nothing comparable exists in tools that just hand you a command.
- **`source`** extends planner's vocabulary: `ai-filtered`,
  `deterministic-fallback` (no provider), `provider-error` (outage → fallback,
  never a dead feature), `no-candidates`.
- **Gates, in order**: 404 assessment/target; 409 when `current_phase` is not a
  plan phase (`PHASE_PROPOSAL_REFUSALS` names the door to use instead — 409 of
  a bare "no" is a worse answer); 409 while a step is running (`status ==
  'running'` — proposing mid-run reasons about a half-written engagement); 409
  at `MAX_PLAN_STEPS`.
- **Proposals are tagged with `current_phase`** and only fire when it is a plan
  phase. This is not a limitation to lift casually: `analyze_assessment` only
  ever reads the CURRENT phase's executions, so a step tagged for a phase the
  assessment is not working in could never be analyzed at all. Crossing phases
  means changing the state machine.
- **Accepting a proposal uses the existing `PUT /plan`** — there is deliberately
  no second write path, so `normalize_plan`, executed-step immutability and
  `MAX_PLAN_STEPS` keep living in one place. The UI merges picked candidates
  into `draftPlan` and the operator hits the ordinary Save plan.
- **Provenance is verified, not trusted**: `driven_by` finding ids that do not
  exist in this assessment are dropped (the cheap analogue of PentestGPT's
  leaf-node verification), and `confidence` is clamped 0-100.
- **Candidate caps**, applied in order: every candidate through the gates, then
  truncated to `MAX_NEXT_CANDIDATES` (6) with the truncation DISCLOSED in
  `notes`, then headroom `MAX_PLAN_STEPS - len(plan)`.
- **`restricted_tools` must be filtered in `next_steps` itself.** The policy
  engine does not know the letter's per-target bans (they are letter-derived,
  not capability rules), but `execute_step` refuses them outright — offering one
  would offer a step that cannot be approved. Same reason `create_assessment`
  drops them at plan time.
- **The new attack surface is real and mitigated**: tool output now reaches a
  model prompt, and nmap banners / HTTP headers are written by the TARGET. That
  is a target-controlled prompt-injection channel. Defences: `strip_ansi` on
  every tool-derived string, per-field and total bounds, a delimited
  `<untrusted_observation>` block with a standing instruction that it is data
  and never a direction, and — decisively — authorization never depends on model
  text (scopes come from the Target row, the policy engine re-checks every
  command). Worst case is a useless proposal, never an out-of-scope step.

## Deployment modes (real-world vs. lab)

- **Execution engines:** `local` (subprocess on the backend host — the Docker
  path, unchanged) and `kali_vm` (the attacker-VM engine). Mode + SSH host/
  port/username/password live in `app_settings`; the password is a
  Fernet-encrypted write-only secret like the proxy password. An incompletely
  configured VM fails closed at the execute endpoint (409), never silently
  falling back to local. `POST /settings/ssh-test` connects, verifies tmux +
  all 7 tools, creates the `redteam` tmux session, and returns the VM's
  attestation (hostname, OS, kernel, SSH host-key SHA256 fingerprint).
- **Kali VM engine mechanics** (`modules/ssh_executor.py`): per-execution
  paramiko connection (password auth, no pooling) → attestation snapshot →
  ensure tmux session `redteam` (220x50, history-limit 100k) → type the
  command with `tmux send-keys -l` + Enter, then queue `echo "__RT_EXIT_<token>__:$?"`
  as a second typed line (the shell runs it when the command finishes; the
  typed echo shows `$?` unexpanded so it can't false-match the exit regex).
  Poll `tmux capture-pane -p -J -S -` ~1 s, diff by prefix (whole-capture
  reset on scrollback churn), stream deltas to the live registry, and trim
  prompts/echoes/marker from the stored stdout. Timeout sends `tmux C-c`.
  One shared pane ⇒ `asyncio.Lock` serializes VM-mode executions. Client-
  disconnect: a `threading.Event` stops the monitor thread and C-c fires via
  a fresh connection. `tool_executions.execution_host` persists the
  attestation per step; UI badges + report "Executed on" line cite it.
- The framework is an **assessor's toolkit**, not a lab-only exhibit. `docker compose up`
  runs backend + frontend only; the operator registers whatever targets the client
  letter authorizes. The demo target — the self-hosted OmniRoute gateway — runs on
  the Windows host itself (see the README's OmniRoute demo section).
- Reachability is the only network requirement: scanners run inside the backend
  container, so the target must be routable from there (Docker DNS name, LAN IP, public
  host, or `host.docker.internal` for the Windows/macOS host itself). On Linux,
  uncomment `network_mode: host` on the backend for accurate LAN subnet scans.
- Proven out of the Docker network: `host.docker.internal` registered as a target,
  one step approved, real output in the live terminal.
- Targets arrive either from the parsed letter ("Set up everything from this letter")
  or by manual registration; either way the policy engine still gates every command
  against the authorized scopes and the letter's per-target tool restrictions.
- Second entry mode (prompt mode): the "New assessment" panel has two tabs —
  "From a letter" (single registered target, letter pre-fills objective/requirements)
  and "Your own prompt" (operator picks multiple registered targets and writes a
  free-text prompt; the UI calls `POST /assessments/` once per target with the prompt
  as the objective). Same planner, same policy review, same per-target restrictions —
  there is no parallel, weaker path. With an AI provider configured the prompt steers
  the drafted commands; without one, each target gets the default deterministic plan.
- Remote/LAN UI access: set `VITE_API_URL` (browser → backend) and
  `CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS` (backend → allowed origin) to the machine's LAN IP.
- Honest scope statement: non-destructive discovery/enumeration plus governance
  (authorization, approval, audit) by design, WITH letter-gated controlled
  exploitation: vulnerability verification with bounded, non-destructive
  proof-of-concepts and a single benign verification record per flaw, only on
  targets whose engagement letter explicitly authorizes it. No privilege
  escalation, no persistence, no lateral movement, no data exfiltration beyond
  that record. That restraint is what makes it safe to point at a paying
  client's production estate.

## Manual effort vs. the framework (the demo's argument)

- Manual: read the letter, hand-translate 7 objectives into ~15–20 commands, remember
  each target's tool restrictions, run them one by one, then spend hours correlating
  output into scored findings and writing the report.
- Framework: letter parsed in seconds, restricted steps dropped automatically with the
  reason shown, 7 approvals, correlated + scored findings, report generated in minutes —
  with a full audit trail no manual run produces for free.
- The letter's final deliverable asks for exactly this evidence, so the comparison is
  part of the engagement, not a marketing aside.

## Fixed bugs (do not reintroduce)

- Parser: reportlab bullets extract via pypdf as `\x7f` (DEL); `BULLET_RE` must match
  it or objectives vanish and intro sentences pollute bullet lists. Items start only
  on bullet lines.
- Planner: never re-prefix a web target — `http://{target}` template + pre-prefixed
  target once produced `http://http://host:3000`.
- Planner: name-resolving tools (nmap/traceroute/dig/nslookup, `HOST_ONLY_TOOLS`) get the
  BARE host; nmap learns the port via `-p`. `nmap juice-shop:3000` and
  `dig +short juice-shop:3000` fail with "Failed to resolve" **and still exit 0**, so a
  whole plan once ran green and produced findings from curl alone. sslscan and the web
  tools parse `host:port` themselves and keep it.
- Analyzer: nuclei severity is compared LOWERCASE. Title-casing it first meant no driver
  branch ever matched, so a Critical CVE match scored the same as an informational one;
  `info` also maps to Low, never Medium.
- Analyzer: `_finding_curl` files header findings only after a real `HTTP/x nnn` status
  line (`STATUS_LINE_RE`), resetting per redirect hop. Without that gate a refused
  connection produced all five "missing security header" findings for a host that was
  never reached (main.py passes no return_code, so the status line is the only signal).
- Analyzer: every stream goes through `strip_ansi` before parsing. whatweb/sslscan/nuclei
  colourise even into a pipe, and the escapes land mid-token (`SSLv3 \x1b[32menabled`,
  `\x1b[1mHTTPServer\x1b[0m[nginx]`), which silently zeroed three parsers. The plan also
  passes `--color=never` / `--no-colour` / `-nc`, but parsing must not depend on it.
- Analyzer: nuclei v3 prints `[template] [http] [severity] url` — the protocol field is
  optional in `NUCLEI_LINE_RE`. nmap's tentative `ppp?` must still yield a finding
  (lower confidence, description says tentative), not be dropped by the regex.
- nuclei is pinned to 3.11.1 in the Dockerfile: 3.1.9 died with `fatal error: concurrent
  map writes` mid-run (exit 2, no findings, 149 s wasted). Do not downgrade.
- Policy engine: a CIDR target is a RANGE, not an address. `normalize_host` strips
  the `/24`, so the old membership check validated a /24 sweep against a /25 scope
  by checking only the base address — authorizing double the range. CIDR targets now
  require `requested.subnet_of(authorized_network)`, never the base-address path.
- Analyzer: `_finding_nmap` tracks `Nmap scan report for …` lines so sweep findings
  carry host-qualified endpoints. Without it every host block's "port 80 open" had
  the same port-only endpoint and the fingerprint dedupe silently merged them —
  every host after the first vanished from the report.
- Executor timeout is 360 s, measured against the nuclei step: 5,242 templates /
  12,078 requests ≈ 308 s at the letter-mandated 30 req/s. A 300 s cap killed the scan
  at 99% complete and stamped every run "timed out". The plan passes `-duc` so runtime
  cannot creep past the cap via template auto-updates — which requires the Dockerfile
  to seed templates at build time (`nuclei -duc -update-templates`), or a fresh
  container dies with "no templates provided for scan". Re-measure before raising
  `-rl` or the timeout.

- Dropzone: whole box is the picker (click + Enter/Space via ref); hidden file input
  clears `e.target.value` after every pick so re-selecting the same file re-fires onChange.
- Report `window.open` runs after an await → popup blockers swallow it; fallback link
  (`reportUrl` state) is rendered when `window.open` returns null.
- Assessment rows are busy-guarded; execute/analyze/report capture the assessment id up
  front so switching assessments mid-request cannot cross-write results.
- Uploads go through `request()` (timeouts + friendly errors), not raw `fetch`.
- `PUT /plan` invalidation: a PURE APPEND (`normalized[:len(previous)] ==
  previous and longer`) invalidates only the appended steps' phases; anything
  else invalidates the whole assessment, as before. The blanket version meant
  accepting one mid-engagement proposal deleted EVERY finding — including the
  earlier phases' findings and the exploitation evidence linking them, the exact
  material the report cites. Appending to the current phase is safe by
  construction: a phase the assessment is working in has, by definition, not
  been analyzed yet, so it has no findings to lose.
- Frontend plan lock is PER STEP, not blanket: `locked = running || executed`.
  The old `planLocked = executions.length > 0` froze the entire plan the moment
  anything ran — including the "Add command" and "Save plan" buttons — which
  contradicted the backend's documented rule ("executed steps are immutable;
  unexecuted steps and later phases stay editable") and made accepting a
  proposal mid-engagement impossible. `planEditable` now requires the executed
  PREFIX to be intact; the backend refuses any change to it regardless.
- `next_steps` must not rely on exact-string dedupe to catch near-duplicates:
  `normalize_command` collapses whitespace/case only, so a candidate with
  different FLAGS for the same tool+target is legitimately offered. That is
  deliberate (a flags-differing re-run can be real deepening) — do not "fix" it
  with a tool+host heuristic, which would suppress legitimate work.

## Demo runbook

### Attacker-VM mode (the credibility demo)

0. Kali VM (one-time): `sudo apt install -y openssh-server tmux nmap traceroute
   dnsutils whatweb sslscan nuclei curl sqlmap metasploit-framework && sudo
   systemctl enable --now ssh` + `git clone --depth 1
   https://gitlab.com/exploit-database/exploitdb.git /opt/exploitdb && sudo
   ln -s /opt/exploitdb/searchsploit /usr/local/bin/searchsploit`
   (+ root SSH or NOPASSWD sudo for traceroute's raw sockets). Windows backend
   needs `pip install paramiko` (in requirements.txt since v2.1).
1. Boot the VirtualBox lab: Kali attacker VM (e.g. 192.168.198.15) attached to
   the same network as the assessment host (the 192.168.198.0/24 lab LAN —
   the host's Wi-Fi address is 192.168.198.86). The letter's primary target —
   the OmniRoute gateway — runs on the Windows host itself at
   192.168.198.86:20128 (server listening on 0.0.0.0, firewall rule for TCP
   20128 scoped to the lab subnet; check with `netstat -ano | findstr :20128`).
   A Metasploitable VM
   works as an extra target: register it manually with
   "controlled exploitation authorized" UNCHECKED (the letter does not name
   it), watch the policy engine refuse the sqlmap step at approval — that
   refusal is the guardrail demo — then register an authorized target to
   show the same command passing.
2. Windows: `uvicorn main:app --port 8000` (backend venv) + `npm run dev`
   (frontend) — no Docker needed in this mode.
3. UI → Configuration → Execution mode = Kali VM over SSH → host/port/user/
   password → **Test connection**: project the attestation card (real OS,
   kernel, host-key fingerprint) — this is the "not hardcoded" anchor.
4. On the Kali console: `tmux attach -t redteam` — leave the VM on screen.
5. Import the letter → set up targets → approve steps: the audience watches
   the framework type each command into the Kali terminal; the UI live
   terminal streams the same output; every audit row and the report carry
   "ran on Kali VM user@host" with the attestation.
6. Phases on the VM engine: recon → analyze → "Draft exploitation plan" →
   the msfconsole scanner steps (VM-only) now run, typed into the tmux
   session, and the phase stepper advances through exploitation and
   post-exploitation with the report citing the VM attestation.
7. Fallback: switch Execution mode back to Local/Docker in Settings.

### Local / Docker mode (the original runbook)

1. `docker compose down -v` (clean DB) then `docker compose up --build`
2. Open http://localhost:5173 → import the PDF in "Read the client's letter"
3. "Set up everything from this letter" → review the plan
4. Demo policy rejection: edit a command dangerously (e.g. `nmap -oN out.txt ...` or
   retarget to google.com) → approve → refused with a clear reason
5. Approve & execute each recon step → live terminal streams output → audit trail
6. Analyze results → the phase stepper advances to Vulnerability analysis
7. "Draft exploitation plan" → searchsploit/sqlmap/curl-PoC steps from the
   findings; msfconsole steps refuse in local mode (VM-only) with the reason —
   demo that refusal too. Approve & execute → analyze → findings now carry
   "✓ verified by exploitation" badges.
8. "Draft post-exploitation plan" → bounded --banner/--current-user steps →
   execute → analyze → Generate report (HTML, cites the engagement, phases,
   and the verification evidence)
9. Real-world proof: add `host.docker.internal:20128` as a target (the
   OmniRoute gateway on the host) and approve a step — the scanners reach a
   machine outside the compose network (nmap resolves it to the host's IP,
   traceroute shows the hop through the Docker gateway).
10. Subnet sweep: register the letter's second target (`192.168.198.0/24`) — the
   2-step discovery plan lists the lab machines (Kali VM, the host) as live
   hosts and files per-host findings (e.g. `192.168.198.86:20128/tcp`). Demo
   the guardrail too: application tools are restricted for the segment, and
   the exploitation gate refuses every exploit-grade command on it (the
   letter authorizes verification for the gateway only).

API gates that look like bugs during a demo but are deliberate: executed steps are
immutable (unexecuted steps and appended phases stay editable), analysis requires
every *enabled* step of the CURRENT phase to have run (disable steps before starting
if you want a shorter run), and a phase's plan can only be drafted once.